<BaseLayout title="Flighted & Scoop Conveyors: Incline Conveying for Bulk & Granular Products" description="Flighted and scoop conveyors: cleated belt incline handling for bulk, granular, and small products. Flight types, incline angles, and food-grade options." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-types/flighted-scoop-conveyors/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema, faqSchema]}>
<h1>Flighted &amp; Scoop Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A flighted conveyor uses raised cleats, flights, or scoop-shaped attachments bonded to a belt surface to move bulk, granular, and small discrete products up inclines and vertical elevations that flat belt conveyors cannot handle. Flights prevent product rollback on steep inclines, and sidewall or bucket designs reach fully vertical lifts. That makes these conveyors the usual answer for elevation changes in packaging lines handling snack foods, granular chemicals, hardware, and loose components.</p>
<h2 id="why-heading">Why Standard Belts Fail on Inclines</h2>
<p>Standard flat belt conveyors lose grip on products as the incline steepens. Gravity pulls product backward, causing rollback, tumbling, and accumulation at the bottom of the incline. The limiting angle depends on the friction between product and belt and on the product's shape, so loose, granular, and round products fail at much lower angles than flat-bottom cartons. A ball bearing rolls back at any positive angle.</p>
<p>Textured and rough-top belts extend the usable incline range for bags, pouches, and cartons, but this is still insufficient for many packaging line layouts that require steeper elevation changes. When floor space is limited, the conveyor must climb steeply over short horizontal distances. A 10-foot elevation change at 15 degrees requires 37 feet of horizontal run. The same elevation change at 45 degrees requires only 10 feet.</p>
<p>Flighted conveyors solve this by adding physical barriers, cleats, flights, sidewalls, or scoops, that trap product between flights and prevent backward movement regardless of incline angle. The flight height, spacing, and profile determine the maximum product size and incline angle the conveyor can handle. For applications requiring vertical elevation without any incline run at all, <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-lift/">vertical lift conveyors</a> provide a dedicated solution.</p>
<h2 id="types-heading">Flight &amp; Cleat Types</h2>
<h3>Standard Cleats (Straight Flights)</h3>
<p>Straight cleats are vertical fins bonded perpendicular to the belt surface, spanning the full belt width. Cleat height is chosen from the product size and the volume each pocket must carry. Spacing between cleats depends on product size, closer spacing for small granular products, wider spacing for larger items. Standard cleats handle moderate to steep inclines for most bulk and granular products, with the limit set by when material starts to cascade over the cleat.</p>
<p>The cleat profile is typically rectangular or T-shaped. T-shaped cleats provide a wider contact surface at the top, creating a larger pocket between cleats. Straight cleats are the simplest and lowest-cost flight option. They work well for uniform granular products (pellets, kibble, hardware) but struggle with irregular shapes that bridge between cleats or roll over them.</p>
<h3>Chevron (V-Pattern) Cleats</h3>
<p>Chevron cleats form a V-shape or herringbone pattern on the belt surface. The V-pattern channels product toward the belt center during incline operation, preventing material from sliding off the belt edges. Chevron belts handle moderate inclines and excel at conveying fine granular materials (sand, sugar, salt, powder) that migrate sideways on standard cleated belts.</p>
<p>The V-angle and cleat height determine the maximum particle size and incline angle. Steeper V-angles provide stronger centering force but reduce the effective belt carrying capacity. Chevron belts are common in aggregate, mining, and agricultural applications but see use in packaging for bulk ingredient handling, moving sugar, flour, or salt from storage to packaging equipment hoppers.</p>
<h3>Sidewall Cleated Belts</h3>
<p>Sidewall belts combine corrugated flexible sidewalls along the belt edges with transverse cleats between the sidewalls. The sidewalls form a continuous trough that prevents material from falling off the belt edges. The cross-cleats prevent rollback. This combination handles steep inclines and fully vertical lifts with bulk materials. Continental describes its <a href="https://allstateconveyors.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/02/Continental_FLEXOWELL%C2%AE_Steep-Angle-Vertical-Conveying-System.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FLEXOWELL sidewall belt system</a> as conveying at any angle from 0° to 90°.</p>
<p>The corrugated sidewall flexes around pulleys at the top and bottom of the conveyor, then springs back to full height on the carrying side. Sidewall and cleat height set the material volume per linear foot. Continental lists FLEXOWELL sidewalls and cleats from 40 mm (about 1.6 inches) up to 630 mm (24.8 inches), with the taller sizes aimed at mining and heavy bulk duty. These belts are critical for moving bulk ingredients, grain, nuts, seeds, powders, to elevated packaging machine hoppers in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food packaging lines</a>.</p>
<h3>Scoop &amp; Bucket Attachments</h3>
<p>Scoop conveyors use curved, bucket-shaped containers attached to a belt or chain at regular intervals. Each scoop picks up product at the bottom, carries it through the vertical or steep-angle section fully enclosed, and dumps it at the top by inverting over the head pulley or by centrifugal discharge. Scoop conveyors handle very steep and fully vertical lifts.</p>
<p>Scoops are molded from food-grade polymers or fabricated from stainless steel. Some designs use interlocking buckets. <a href="https://www.unitrak.com/equipment/tiptrak" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UniTrak's TipTrak bucket elevator</a>, for example, links food-grade plastic buckets on a rubber chain so product stays contained through horizontal and vertical runs from a single drive and discharges only at the set discharge point. Throughput depends on scoop size, spacing, and speed, and suppliers engineer it from the product's bulk density, flow characteristics, and required rate.</p>
<p>Scoop conveyors protect fragile products better than open cleated belts because the product rides inside an enclosed container rather than pressing against a flat cleat. This makes scoop conveyors preferred for delicate snack foods (chips, crackers), confectionery, and coated products that break or lose coating on cleated belt surfaces.</p>
