<p>Incline limits depend on product friction, shape, and flight geometry. Confirm the rated angle for your product with the supplier. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for pricing factors.</p>
<h2 id="design-heading">Design Considerations</h2>
<h3>Flight Height &amp; Spacing</h3>
<p>Flight height must exceed the largest product dimension that faces the cleat. For granular materials, flight height determines the depth of each pocket, and therefore the volume the conveyor carries per linear foot. Spacing between flights depends on product size: granular materials need closely spaced flights to prevent material from cascading over the cleat at steep angles. Larger discrete items need wider spacing to fit between flights.</p>
<p>Too-tall flights on too-steep inclines create dead zones where product packs against the flight face and does not discharge cleanly at the top. The optimal flight height-to-spacing ratio depends on the product's angle of repose, the steepest angle at which the material rests without sliding. Products with low angles of repose (free-flowing powders, ball bearings) need taller flights and closer spacing than cohesive materials (wet granules, sticky candy).</p>
<h3>Belt Speed on Inclines</h3>
<p>Belt speed decreases as incline angle increases. At steep angles, product sits in the pocket between flights with less gravitational stability. Higher belt speeds generate centrifugal force at the head pulley that can throw product past the discharge point. Flighted incline conveyors usually run slower than flat transport conveyors. Ask the supplier for the maximum speed at your incline and product. Reducing speed decreases throughput, so the belt width and flight spacing must compensate.</p>
<h3>Motor Sizing for Inclines</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">Incline conveyors</a> require more motor power than horizontal conveyors of the same length because the motor must lift product against gravity. Motor horsepower increases with incline angle, belt speed, and product weight per foot. The lift component of the load is the product weight times the vertical rise, so the extra power grows with height and throughput. Give the supplier the rise, product weight per hour, and speed so they can size the drive. VFD control is standard for flighted incline conveyors to allow speed adjustment for different products and to provide controlled soft starts that prevent product surge at startup.</p>
<h3>Infeed &amp; Discharge Design</h3>
<p>The infeed section of a flighted conveyor is typically horizontal or slightly inclined to allow product to load evenly between flights. A hopper, vibratory feeder, or upstream conveyor meters product onto the belt at a controlled rate. Overloading causes product to cascade over flight tops and spill. The discharge section at the top uses a nose-over (small-diameter head pulley) to dump product cleanly off the belt. A scraper or brush at the head pulley removes product residue from the belt surface and flight faces before the return run.</p>
<p>For applications feeding product into weighers, <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">collating systems</a>, or packaging machine hoppers, the discharge must deliver product at a consistent, controllable rate. A vibratory tray or metering conveyor at the discharge point converts the pulsed delivery (one flight at a time) into a continuous stream.</p>
<h2 id="sanitary-heading">Food-Grade &amp; Sanitary Designs</h2>
<p>Food-grade flighted conveyors require specific design features that prevent bacterial harborage and allow thorough cleaning. The flights must be integrally molded into the belt, not mechanically fastened with screws, rivets, or clips that create crevices for bacteria and allergens. Integrally molded flights eliminate the seam between the flight and belt surface.</p>
<p>Belt materials for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary food applications</a> include polyurethane, silicone (high-temperature bakery applications), and antimicrobial formulations that inhibit bacterial growth on the belt surface. Ask the belt supplier which FDA regulation the belt compound meets and for which foods. For example, <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/177.1680" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR 177.1680</a> covers polyurethane resins as food-contact surfaces for bulk quantities of dry food. Blue or white belt colors provide visual contrast for detecting product contamination, a blue belt fragment in white flour is immediately visible.</p>
<p>Frame construction uses 304 or 316 stainless steel with fully welded joints, rounded corners (no sharp edges that trap debris), and open-frame designs that allow spray-through cleaning. Motors for washdown areas carry ingress ratings matched to the cleaning method, with IP69K specified for high-pressure, high-temperature washdown. Ask suppliers for documentation behind any USDA or 3-A claim rather than accepting the label.</p>
<p>Tool-free belt removal is a critical sanitary feature. The belt must lift off the conveyor for cleaning, inspection, and replacement without tools. Quick-release tensioners and removable tail pulleys let one operator remove and reinstall the belt quickly. This encourages thorough daily cleaning and reduces the temptation to skip belt removal during sanitation.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Packaging Line Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Snack food elevation:</strong> Chips, pretzels, crackers, and popcorn move from processing equipment at floor level to elevated packaging machine hoppers using scoop conveyors. The enclosed scoop protects fragile snack products from breakage during the elevation change.</p>
<p><strong>Granular ingredient feeding:</strong> Sugar, salt, spices, and powdered ingredients elevate from bulk storage bins to weigh-fill packaging machines using sidewall cleated belt conveyors. The continuous sidewall prevents spillage, and the belt delivers a steady flow to the hopper. <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Indexing control</a> on the flighted conveyor meters exact volumes to the weigher.</p>
<p><strong>Hardware and small parts:</strong> Fasteners, caps, closures, and small components elevate from vibratory bowls or bulk bins to packaging stations. Standard <a href="/conveyor-types/cleated-conveyors/">cleated belt conveyors</a> handle these applications on moderate to steep inclines. The cleats prevent small round items from rolling back down the incline.</p>
<p><strong>Pharmaceutical capsules and tablets:</strong> Scoop conveyors gently elevate finished capsules and tablets from tablet presses to bottle-filling machines. The enclosed scoop prevents dust generation and cross-contamination. Stainless steel scoops and enclosed conveyor housings meet pharmaceutical cGMP requirements.</p>
<p><strong>Reclaim and recycling:</strong> Flighted conveyors reclaim trim scrap, offcuts, and waste material from packaging operations and elevate it to recycling bins or regrind equipment. The flights handle irregular shapes and mixed materials that flat belt conveyors cannot grip on inclines.</p>
<p>For vertical elevation without any incline run, see <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-lift/">vertical lift conveyors</a>. For accumulating product before or after elevation changes, see <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyors</a>.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Selecting a Flighted Conveyor</h2>
<p>Match the flight type to five parameters:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Incline angle required.</strong> Calculate the angle from the available floor space and elevation change. At gentle angles, a rough-top belt may suffice for bagged or boxed product. Very steep or vertical lifts point toward scoop or sidewall conveyors.</li>
<li><strong>Product characteristics.</strong> Free-flowing granular products need tall flights with close spacing. Fragile products need enclosed scoops. Sticky products need flights with non-stick coatings or release agents. Round products need flights tall enough to prevent rolling over the top.</li>
<li><strong>Throughput requirement.</strong> Higher throughput requires wider belts, taller flights, or faster speeds. Speed is limited by the incline angle, so width and flight height are the primary throughput levers on steep inclines.</li>
<li><strong>Sanitary requirements.</strong> <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and pharmaceutical applications</a> require integrally molded flights, FDA-compliant materials, stainless steel construction, and tool-free belt removal. These features add cost but are non-negotiable for regulated products.</li>
<li><strong>Discharge method.</strong> Products feeding into hoppers need a controlled discharge rate. Products feeding onto downstream conveyors need clean separation from the flight at the head pulley. The head pulley diameter, scraper design, and discharge chute geometry must match the product and the downstream equipment.</li>
</ol>
<p>Cost drivers for flighted and scoop conveyors are belt or bucket construction, sidewall height, incline and lift height, sanitary construction, and the infeed and discharge equipment. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for detailed pricing factors.</p>
